cw: yes. Today a server publishes by writing the object onto its own job (jobs.update with app), and the pages fish it out of the job updates as job.app. With an events family that becomes events.publish, a second verb, and the readers take an app event instead of digging in jobs.

Who publishes today:
- chatClerver, deskAgent, deskVerify, fileServer (spirit.jobs.publish);
- appServer's stream publisher, through which the desk server publishes;
- spirit.js's spirit.jobs.publish itself, and the kernel's old one.

Who reads it on the pages:
- the old shell (it hands each job's app to the app it belongs to);
- the desk's translator into the cache (shell/js/desk/data.js);
- the Jobs app.

So the family gets two verbs, events.subscribe and events.publish {app object}, and these 10 files switch, on top of the 7 above.
